Dockerization and Deployment Steps
Step 1: Create a Dockerfile

First, I created a simple Dockerfile for the Node.js application.

The initial Dockerfile was created to install the required dependencies and run the application inside a Docker container.

Step 2: Build the Docker Image

After creating the Dockerfile, I ran the following command to check whether the image could be built successfully:

docker build -t bestbags-app .

This was used to verify that the Dockerfile and application dependencies were configured correctly.

Step 3: Add the Build Command

Initially, I added the following instruction to the Dockerfile:

RUN npm run build

However, the Docker build failed with an error.

I investigated the error and checked the package.json file. Under the scripts section, there was no build script available.

For example, the project did not contain:

"scripts": {
    "build": "..."
}

Therefore, npm run build could not be executed.

Step 4: Remove the Build Step

Since the application did not have a build script, I removed:

RUN npm run build

from the Dockerfile.

I then built the image again:

docker build -t bestbags-app .

This time, the Docker image was built successfully.

Step 5: Create the Docker Compose File

After successfully building the application image, I created a docker-compose.yml file.

The Docker Compose configuration included:

The Node.js application
MongoDB
Port configuration
Docker network
Persistent volume for MongoDB
Environment variables

For MongoDB, I used the latest MongoDB image:

image: mongo:latest

The MongoDB service was configured to use port:

27017

The application was configured to use port:

3000

Both containers were placed on the same Docker network so that the application could communicate with MongoDB.

Step 6: Remove the Existing Image and Start Docker Compose

I removed the existing application image and started the services using:

docker compose up

The containers started, but the application returned an error.

Step 7: Configure the Backend URI

The error occurred because the application did not have the required URI/configuration to communicate with the backend.

I investigated the error and identified that the required URI needed to be provided through the application's environment configuration.

Step 8: Add Environment Variables

I added the required .env file and configured the required values, including:

MongoDB username
MongoDB password
Database name
Backend/application URI
Other required environment variables

I also added the required Docker Compose dependency configuration so that the application service depends on the MongoDB service.

For example:

depends_on:
  - mongodb

I then ran:

docker compose up
Step 9: Container Starts Successfully

After adding the required environment variables and dependency configuration, the containers started successfully.

I then accessed the application through:

http://localhost:3000

However, another error was displayed.

Step 10: Investigate the Secret Error

The application returned an error indicating that a secret was required.

I investigated the error and found that the application expected a secret value to be provided.

The secret could be a custom value, but it needed to be provided as a string.

Step 11: Add the Secret

I added the required secret to the .env file.

For example:

SECRET=my-secret-value

Then I passed the value to the application through the Docker Compose environment configuration.

Example:

environment:
  SECRET: ${SECRET}

This allows Docker Compose to read the value from the .env file and provide it to the application container.

Step 12: Run Docker Compose Again

After adding the secret, I started the application again:

docker compose up

The containers started successfully.

When I accessed:

http://localhost:3000

the application produced another error.

Step 13: Investigate the Category Error

The new error indicated that categories such as:

Category [0] not found
Category [1] not found
Category [2] not found

were missing.

I investigated the application and identified that the MongoDB database was empty.

The application expected initial product/category data to already exist in the database, but the MongoDB container had started with an empty database.

The project contains a seedDB script that can be used to populate the database with the required initial data.

Step 14: Create a Docker Entrypoint Script

To automatically initialize the database when the container starts, I created:

docker-entrypoint.sh

The entrypoint script is responsible for running the database seed operation before starting the application.

I then updated the Dockerfile to:

Copy docker-entrypoint.sh into the container.
Give the script execute permission.
Configure the script as the container's entrypoint.

For example:

COPY docker-entrypoint.sh /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh

RUN chmod +x /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh"]

The entrypoint script can then perform the required database initialization and start the Node.js application.

Step 15: Rebuild and Start the Containers

Because the Dockerfile was modified, the existing image needed to be rebuilt.

I used:

docker compose up --build

The --build option forces Docker Compose to rebuild the application image using the updated Dockerfile before starting the containers.

At this stage, the complete flow is:

User
  |
  | http://localhost:3000
  v
Frontend / Node.js Application
  |
  | MongoDB connection
  v
MongoDB :27017
  |
  | Database initialization
  v
Seed Data

The application now has the required configuration, secret, database connection, and initial database data required for the application to run correctly.